{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ef3575992a010dd5","type":"file","file":"00 Notes/How To Be A Stoic.md","x":0,"y":0,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"6607f9119ab0e3a6","type":"text","text":"[[Stoicism]] - [[Stoicism.canvas]]\n\nBasically: we can’t control everything in the world. \n\nHowever, we can control how we react.\n\nBy acting indifferent in extreme situations, we can develop ourselves to be happy and nicer to others.","x":500,"y":109,"width":540,"height":231},
		{"id":"0b1d754701ee7098","type":"text","text":"[[The Four Virtues of Stoicism]]","x":1210,"y":480,"width":380,"height":60},
		{"id":"14a45ec7ef678f38","type":"text","text":"[[Kindness]]","x":-340,"y":715,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"2d4a804ff23c2669","type":"text","text":"**Wisdom**\n- Education\n- Constant pursuit of knowledge throughout life\n- Willingness to listen to others’ opinions and feedback","x":1960,"y":278,"width":436,"height":230},
		{"id":"87182defb463784f","type":"text","text":"**Courage**\n- Being brave\n- Standing up for what you believe in\n- Doing crazy stuff (e.g. skydiving)","x":1960,"y":560,"width":470,"height":180},
		{"id":"fd52678e593ca7fb","type":"text","text":"**Temperance**\n- Balancing the other virtues\n- Not too much of anything (e.g. don’t eat crazily until you are full)\n- [[don't make a big deal of things]]","x":1940,"y":820,"width":706,"height":167},
		{"id":"91d968b1a098bbbd","type":"text","text":"**Justice**\n- Treating others with respect, even if they have done wrong\n- Treating everyone else fairly and equally nomatter what you personal prejudice or dislike is.","x":1969,"y":1117,"width":751,"height":143},
		{"id":"8aeaa8fa3a754b19","type":"text","text":"[[Lexigraphic Preferences]] are part of making decisions. Where humans put certain things in indisguinshable categories.\n\nExample: You would trade $20 for a burger, but you would never trade your mother for a burger.","x":-435,"y":1400,"width":540,"height":242},
		{"id":"afd83ca0e0c04ac6","type":"text","text":"For a Stoic, *nothing* can be traded if it compromises your character ([[Integrity]]","x":-980,"y":1400,"width":419,"height":88},
		{"id":"082592fdab90d8f0","type":"text","text":"Key People","x":-361,"y":-242,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5bc578272a3a2c1f","type":"text","text":"[[Epictetus]] is a core figure in the book. Pigliucci constantly refers to quotes by Epictetus, analyses them deeply and acts as if epictetus was his teacher who he “often talked to\".”","x":-1040,"y":-880,"width":430,"height":140},
		{"id":"8ca6470685c50e0a","type":"text","text":"Concept: [[Think before you act (Epictetus)]]\n\n> “we should act in order, with due consideration, and with proper care.” \n> Epictetus - pp.23\n\n\nAccording to Epictetus, we should “act in order,” conveying the message of undertaking every [[No action undertaken without aim|action purposefully with a clear aim]] to ensure you complete your goals. In real life, we can combat procastination by acting in order e.g. shutting your laptop the second you feel lazy or unproductive- → lie on beanbag and read a book / go for a walk and listen to music.","x":-2100,"y":-549,"width":723,"height":307},
		{"id":"6643d0c7c7aa4a14","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260702125454.png","x":-1677,"y":-1600,"width":311,"height":400},
		{"id":"13c690d18e9ed936","type":"text","text":"[[James Stockdale]] was a American general who served in the Vietnam war as a general and POW. Despite torture, he followed [[Epictetus]]’s teaching for comfort.","x":-2135,"y":-877,"width":758,"height":137},
		{"id":"664a284640d77df6","type":"text","text":"”Do this with every act” - pp.211","x":3640,"y":173,"width":300,"height":105},
		{"id":"e354dec145a06da9","type":"text","text":"# Application","x":820,"y":1733,"width":318,"height":56},
		{"id":"aa643012b0478c9b","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> Their emphasis was on how real people behave, not just how they talk.\n\nTo be a stoic, you actively must apply the Stoic concepts (virtues, ideas, philosophy) in every opportunity presented to you everyday.","x":1501,"y":1659,"width":678,"height":204},
		{"id":"9a285615c80fe7e2","type":"text","text":"Example:\nAt the gym, be **courageous**, work hard during each set.","x":1680,"y":2026,"width":562,"height":119},
		{"id":"652892e9b6fb4270","type":"text","text":"**APPLICATION**\nEvery night when you journal:\n- Write down minimum 3 ways you demonstrated Stoicism today\n- Example: Stubbed my toe but took deep breaths.","x":1800,"y":2245,"width":503,"height":201},
		{"id":"404f4c2f9e080d15","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “Do the right thing, the rest doesn’t matter.”\n> - Marcus Aurelius - 6.2\n\nJust do the right thing, be just and be honest everyday in real life.","x":1678,"y":2665,"width":888,"height":255},
		{"id":"140f560f3b5d07e4","type":"text","text":"> [!QUOTE]\n> “Epictetus tells us to use every occassion, every challenge, as a way to exercise our virtue, to become a better human being by constant application.”\n\nThe only way to apply Stoic principles is by performing, practicing and refining them every day.\n> ","x":1322,"y":1320,"width":730,"height":205},
		{"id":"19926e2f63334942","type":"text","text":"[[Logos]]\n\nThe universe is not here by chance, instead it is an intelligent entity.\n\nWe must follow *Logos* by letting fate guide us and following nature","x":1141,"y":-860,"width":449,"height":218},
		{"id":"98d9ae8629e04c3e","type":"text","text":"[[Logotherapy]] is a therapy created by [[Viktor E. Frankl]] which is a method of finding meaning in one’s life as a way to build happiness.\n\nYou can achieve meaning by:\n1. Work/Deed (e.g. you love what you do and are passionate)\n2. Love/Someone special (You have found someone who you feel deep connection and meaning to)\n3. Through great suffering (e.g. Stockdale in Vietnam war)","x":1921,"y":-960,"width":796,"height":238},
		{"id":"8ebf140749f3c089","type":"text","text":"[[Dichotomy|Dichotomy of control]]\n\n- We cannot control everything in the world (e.g. other people, weather)\n- Therefore, there is no use in getting sad, or [[If you cannot control everything, what is the use of being gloomy?|gloomy]] about it","x":1210,"y":-53,"width":480,"height":218},
		{"id":"9c5d4b30fa4a3b61","type":"text","text":"When people say, “It’s none of your business.” \nThey are absolutely correct, it *is* none of your business because it is out of your control. \n\nTherefore, don’t be saddened.","x":1814,"y":-540,"width":763,"height":133},
		{"id":"168a8641807a3649","type":"text","text":"[[The Dog Analogy for the Dichotomy of Control]]","x":1960,"y":-252,"width":498,"height":74},
		{"id":"0eb7833918f2b282","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260708194706.png","x":2761,"y":-407,"width":400,"height":382},
		{"id":"dbc48b7a7449def9","type":"text","text":"**The reserve clause**\n- If something interrupts your plans, you must expect this and not feel to mad.\n\n> [!QUOTE] \n> “I want a bath, but at the same time, I want to keep my will aligned with nature.\n\nBy doing this, we can feel less annoyed when things ‘don’t go our way’","x":2458,"y":78,"width":919,"height":293},
		{"id":"bcf29c8eb9b3ffc7","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260705152626.png","x":-1800,"y":141,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"20814e3fc10ca2fe","type":"text","text":"[[oikeiôsis]]\nAncient greek for “appropriation of” or “familiarzation”\n\nPrinciple of treating others’ concerns/problems as your own.\n\nEmphasises that we need to move further from just our ‘self’. We need to actively engage with family, friends and even strangers and always be nice.","x":-1080,"y":258,"width":443,"height":283},
		{"id":"07e8dfff10940cd0","type":"text","text":"Your soul (meaning [[Integrity]]) is most important. Then your body (health), then family, then community, etc.\n\nBe kind to everyone.","x":-1800,"y":602,"width":489,"height":173},
		{"id":"0857ac5cd75aa9af","type":"text","text":"**Speak little and well–let others talk for you**\n\n> [!QUOTE]\n> “Let silence be the goal for the most part; say what is only necessary and be brief about it.”\n> [[How To Be A Stoic]] - pp.219\n\nOthers much more appreciate it when you listen to *them* instead of the opposite way round. Always perform [[Active Listening]] and don’t talk too much.","x":-1328,"y":780,"width":691,"height":315},
		{"id":"1b2fa2d04a512ec0","type":"text","text":"[[Explaining what Stoicism is]]","x":1639,"y":3034,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"0d495a67ae5e8903","x":-1925,"y":938,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"[[Your goal is to listen]] - essay i wrote on THIS QUOTE"}
	],
